DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ggssttrriippee utility is used for setting up a stripe on two or more disks.
     The striped device can be configured using two different methods:
     “manual” or “automatic”.  When using the “manual” method, no metadata are
     stored on the devices, so the striped device has to be configured by hand
     every time it is needed.  The “automatic” method uses on-disk metadata to
     detect devices.  Once devices are labeled, they will be automatically de‐
     tected and configured.

     The first argument to ggssttrriippee indicates an action to be performed:

     ccrreeaattee   Set up a striped device from the given devices with specified
              _n_a_m_e.  This is the “manual” method and the stripe will not exist
              after a reboot (see _D_E_S_C_R_I_P_T_I_O_N above).  The kernel module
              _g_e_o_m___s_t_r_i_p_e_._k_o will be loaded if it is not loaded already.

     llaabbeell    Set up a striped device from the given devices with the speci‐
              fied _n_a_m_e.  This is the “automatic” method, where metadata are
              stored in every device's last sector.  The kernel module
              _g_e_o_m___s_t_r_i_p_e_._k_o will be loaded if it is not loaded already.

     ssttoopp     Turn off an existing striped device by its _n_a_m_e.  This command
              does not touch on-disk metadata!

     ddeessttrrooyy  Same as ssttoopp.

     cclleeaarr    Clear metadata on the given devices.

     dduummpp     Dump metadata stored on the given devices.

     lliisstt     See geom(8).

     ssttaattuuss   See geom(8).

     llooaadd     See geom(8).

     uunnllooaadd   See geom(8).

     Additional options:

     --ff             Force the removal of the specified striped device.

     --hh             Hardcode providers' names in metadata.

     --ss _s_t_r_i_p_e_s_i_z_e  Specifies size of stripe block in bytes.  The _s_t_r_i_p_e_s_i_z_e
                    must be a multiple of the largest sector size of all the
                    providers.

     --vv             Be more verbose.

SSYYSSCCTTLL VVAARRIIAABBLLEESS
     The following sysctl(8) variables can be used to control the behavior of
     the SSTTRRIIPPEE GEOM class.  The default value is shown next to each variable.

     _k_e_r_n_._g_e_o_m_._s_t_r_i_p_e_._d_e_b_u_g: 0
             Debug level of the SSTTRRIIPPEE GEOM class.  This can be set to a num‐
             ber between 0 and 3 inclusive.  If set to 0 minimal debug infor‐
             mation is printed, and if set to 3 the maximum amount of debug
             information is printed.

     _k_e_r_n_._g_e_o_m_._s_t_r_i_p_e_._f_a_s_t: 0
             If set to a non-zero value enable “fast mode” instead of the nor‐
             mal “economic mode”.  Compared to “economic mode”, “fast mode”
             uses more memory, but it is much faster for smaller stripe sizes.
             If enough memory cannot be allocated, SSTTRRIIPPEE will fall back to
             “economic mode”.

     _k_e_r_n_._g_e_o_m_._s_t_r_i_p_e_._m_a_x_m_e_m: 13107200
             Maximum amount of memory that can be consumed by “fast mode” (in
             bytes).  This sysctl(8) variable is read-only and can only be set
             as a tunable in loader.conf(5).

     _k_e_r_n_._g_e_o_m_._s_t_r_i_p_e_._f_a_s_t___f_a_i_l_e_d
             A count of how many times “fast mode” has failed due to an insuf‐
             ficient amount of memory.  If this value is large, you should
             consider increasing the _k_e_r_n_._g_e_o_m_._s_t_r_i_p_e_._m_a_x_m_e_m value.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     Exit status is 0 on success, and 1 if the command fails.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     The following example shows how to set up a striped device from four
     disks with a 128KB stripe size for automatic configuration, create a file
     system on it, and mount it:

           gstripe label -v -s 131072 data /dev/da0 /dev/da1 /dev/da2 /dev/da3
           newfs /dev/stripe/data
           mount /dev/stripe/data /mnt
           [...]
           umount /mnt
           gstripe stop data
           gstripe unload

CCOOMMPPAATTIIBBIILLIITTYY
     The ggssttrriippee interleave is in number of bytes, unlike ccdconfig(8) which
     use the number of sectors.  A ccdconfig(8) _i_l_e_a_v_e of ‘128’ is 64 KB (128
     512B sectors).  The same stripe interleave would be specified as ‘65536’
     for ggssttrriippee.

